<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  =============================================================================
  The Aquarius desktop's right-click menu (labwc)
  =============================================================================
  (An XML comment may not contain two hyphens in a row, which is why the rules
  in this file are drawn with = signs. It is a quirk of XML, not a change of
  style.)

  Right-clicking the empty desktop opens this menu. rc.xml, next to this file,
  binds a right-click on the root to "ShowMenu root-menu"; this file is what
  root-menu contains.

  THIS FILE IS THE FIX FOR THE 6 SEPTEMBER 2026 BENCH BUG, where right-clicking
  the desktop offered Terminal / Reconfigure / Exit. It is worth being exact
  about why, because the obvious explanation is wrong: labwc's own defaults
  ALREADY send a right-click on the desktop to a menu called root-menu. Nothing
  declared one, so labwc used its built-in fallback. Declaring it here is what
  makes the desktop's menu ours.

  =============================================================================
  CHANGE ONE, CHANGE BOTH
  =============================================================================
  This is the AquariusOS copy of session/labwc/menu.xml in the aquarius-shell
  repository, the same rule the `autostart` file next to it already states.
  AquariusOS does NOT stage the shell's session/labwc folder: it ships its own
  hand-maintained copies here, because the two-compositors question is settled
  differently in the OS (labwc, standing decision 2b) and the OS copies carry
  OS-only corrections. So a change to the menu in one repository has to be made
  in the other by hand, or the desktop and the shell drift apart.

  =============================================================================
  THE ONE OS-ONLY DIFFERENCE: `env XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=GNOME` ON SETTINGS
  =============================================================================
  Every gnome-control-center line below is prefixed with
  `env XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=GNOME`. The shell's copy is not, and it must be here.

  gnome-control-center refuses to start unless it believes it is running under
  GNOME. Its own code (shell/cc-application.c, is_supported_desktop()) reads
  XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, splits it on colons, and unless one of the parts is
  exactly "GNOME" or "Unity" it prints

      Running gnome-control-center is only supported under GNOME and Unity,
      exiting

  and exits 1. The Aquarius session exports XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP="Aquarius:wlroots"
  and that line MUST stay as it is: the portals key off it, and renaming the whole
  session to GNOME would send every portal request to GNOME's back ends and break
  screen recording. So the lie is told PER LAUNCH, to one program, and nothing
  else in the session sees it.

  `env` is a real program at /usr/bin/env, so this works whether labwc runs the
  command through a shell or hands the words straight to execvp. A bare
  `XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=GNOME gnome-control-center` would only work in the first
  case, which is why it is not written that way.

  The same fix is applied to org.gnome.Settings.desktop and to the D-Bus
  service file in build_files/55-aquarius-session.sh, so that the dock icon and
  D-Bus activation get it too. See that script for the full reasoning.

  =============================================================================
  WHY THIS IS A labwc MENU AND NOT A SHELL-DRAWN ONE
  =============================================================================
  The shell draws its OWN menu off the Aquarius mark (components/bar/LogoMenu.qml)
  because that one hangs off a bar item the shell owns. The DESKTOP root is the
  compositor's, not the shell's, and there is no standard way for a layer-shell
  client to be told "the user right-clicked the wallpaper here" and to place a
  popup there. labwc's own menu is the right tool for a right-click on labwc's
  own root.

  =============================================================================
  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
  =============================================================================
  Desktop icons. Royce chose a clean desktop; this is only a context menu.
  Left-clicking the desktop still does nothing, on purpose.

  NO COLOURS. Same rule as rc.xml: labwc draws this menu with its own
  Openbox-style theme, and it would be easy to tint it here. Colour in the
  Aquarius Desktop lives in the shell's theme/Ice.qml and theme/Midnight.qml and
  nowhere else.

  Format reference: labwc-menu(5), installed on this machine.
  =============================================================================
-->

<openbox_menu>
  <menu id="root-menu" label="Aquarius">

    <!-- The Aquarius search palette, the same message Super+Space sends. -->
    <item label="Search">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>qs ipc call search toggle</command>
      </action>
    </item>

    <separator />

    <!-- The full Settings app, and its wallpaper page directly. `background` is
         a stable gnome-control-center panel id. The `env` prefix is explained
         at the top of this file and is NOT optional in AquariusOS. -->
    <item label="System Settings">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>env XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=GNOME gnome-control-center</command>
      </action>
    </item>

    <item label="Change Wallpaper">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>env XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=GNOME gnome-control-center background</command>
      </action>
    </item>

    <separator />

    <!-- Leave the session. This is labwc's own Exit, the SAME teardown
         Super+Shift+E runs (see rc.xml) and not a second way out. That matters:
         the launcher outlives labwc precisely so it can clean up afterwards, and
         a different exit route would skip the clean-up and bounce the next
         login off the login screen. -->
    <item label="Log Out">
      <action name="Exit" />
    </item>

    <item label="Sleep">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>systemctl suspend</command>
      </action>
    </item>

    <item label="Restart">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>systemctl reboot</command>
      </action>
    </item>

    <item label="Power Off">
      <action name="Execute">
        <command>systemctl poweroff</command>
      </action>
    </item>

  </menu>
</openbox_menu>
